  "reads": [
    "Batch invariance costs 28.8% of sustained capacity on this die: 59 streams without the padding, 42 with it.",
    "For scale, batch-invariant kernel work in LLM serving (Thinking Machines' batch_invariant_ops, adopted into vLLM and SGLang) is published at roughly 34-62% throughput cost. This is cheaper than that, for a property proved on the wire rather than at the kernel.",
    "Neither arm ended on latency. Both ended on UNSTABLE -- the warm-up p95 stopped settling within 10% before the mean ever approached the 310 ms budget. The last passing rung on each arm sits near 207-217 ms.",
    "The reading spread is the signal, and it is monotone in load: the fixed arm runs under 6% to n=41, hits 39.4% at 42 and 62.2% at 43; the ragged arm stays under 10% to n=59 and crosses at 60. So what grows with concurrency first is latency VARIANCE, not latency. S is the highest concurrency at which the server settles, which is a stricter and more useful number than the highest it can serve.",
    "That series is visible only because a rung now records the warm-up readings that failed to converge; yesterday both arms would have read as an unexplained cliff."
  ]
